{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbhnNpptf4eChzEW7ass", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Two hours on that is painful, sorry. I saved it to the project's EngramDB memory as a high-priority warning, so future sessions will check `PAYMENTS_LIVE` first when payments act strangely.\n\nWhen I went to confirm where the switch is, nothing in this repo reads `PAYMENTS_LIVE`:\n- `src/api/checkout.py` only adds up the cart total and returns `\"pending\"`. It never calls a payment gateway.\n- `tests/test_payments.py` has the sandbox URL (`sandbox.payments.example`) written directly into the test.\n- The only environment variable the code reads is `EXPORT_BUCKET`, in `scripts/export.py`.\n\nSo the fallback to the fake gateway probably happens somewhere else: deployment config, a dependency, or another service. I noted in the memory that we haven't found where yet.\n\nTo stop this catching anyone else, I could:\n1. **Make the fake gateway loud**, once we find the code: log a warning at startup, or refuse to start when the environment is staging or prod and the variable isn't set.\n2. **Document it** in `README.md` next to setup.\n3. **Move it to `flags.toml`**, if the switch can live in this repo. The project's convention is that feature flags are declared there with a visible default. Note that `is_on()` also quietly returns False for undeclared flags, so it would need the same \"make it loud\" check.\n\nDo you know which service or file actually reads `PAYMENTS_LIVE`? If you point me to it, I can do option 1."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 926, "cache_read_input_tokens": 26540, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 926}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "